{"page_content": "\u2022 Conclusions (applicability)\n\nR.11.4.1.1 Persistence assessment (P and vP)\n\nR.11.4.1.1.1 Integrated assessment and testing strategy (ITS) for persistence assessment\n\nA strategy for degradation assessment and testing in the context of PBT/vPvB assessment is proposed in Figure R.11\u20143. A tiered approach to assessment and testing is necessary until a definitive conclusion on persistence can be drawn.\n\nAvailable data consisting solely of screening information can be employed to derive a conclusion mainly for \u201cnot P and not vP\u201d or \u201cmay fulfil the P or vP criteria\u201d. If screening information indicates that the substance \u201cmay fulfil the P or vP criteria\u201d, higher tier information generally needs to be made available.\n\nAppropriate data need to be available to conclude the P/vP-assessment with a conclusion \u201cnot P/vP\u201d on all three compartments (or five, with marine compartments): water (marine water), sediment (marine sediment) and soil. Either the available data, including in normal case simulation test data from one or two compartments, can be interpreted so that a conclusion can be derived on the remaining compartment(s) for which no higher tier data are available, or data need to be available directly on all compartments, or there is another justification for why a conclusion does not need to be drawn for all three (five) compartments.\n\nIn the opposite situation, if a conclusion \u201cP\u201d or \u201cvP\u201d is reached for one compartment, no further testing or assessment of persistence of other environmental compartments is normally necessary. In certain cases it may be possible to draw a conclusion \u201cP\u201d or \u201cvP\u201d based on screening information (e.g. tests on inherent biodegradation) combined with other useful information in a Weight-of-Evidence approach, as described later in this section and indicated in the ITS in Figure R.11\u20143.\n\nScreening test(s) indicating \u201cnot P and not vP\u201d may not be sufficient to conclude that a substance is not persistent, if there is contradicting information on persistence. In this case, additional information (e.g. QSAR, literature studies, read across) and quality of the screening tests should be considered in a Weight-of-Evidence assessment (see also Section R.7.9.4.1 in Chapter R.7b of the Guidance on IR&CSA). The results of the higher tier degradation simulation studies (Annex XIII, Section 3.2: assessment information) are to be given more weight in the Weight-of-Evidence assessment than the screening studies (Annex XIII, section 3.1: screening information). In the presence of a reliable higher tier study, it is not necessary to analyse in detail the reasons for potentially inconsistent outcomes of the screening tests. The outcomes of a reliable and relevant higher tier study, supersede the screening tests.\n\nFor substances containing multiple constituents, impurities and/or additives, the guidance provided below apply to that/those \u201cpart(s)\u201d of the substance, which is/are the target(s) of the assessment and testing. The criteria for selecting an appropriate assessment approach is provided in Section R.11.4.2.2.\n\n1. Substance is readily\n\nbiodegradable?\n\nyes\n\nNot P/vP*\n\nT\n\nno\n\n2. Screening information (Table R.11\u20144): Positive enhanced ready Specific inherent test positive biodegradation test and other Not P and not vP* with non-adapted inoculum data supporting? Specific inherent Negative enhanced ready Potentially P and vP biodegradation test negative biodegradation test? 3. Other information useful in a weight-of-evidence approach: In situ/field Other (testing Simulation test Applicable Monitoring Abiotic degradation and non-testing data degradation QSARs results information) study results\n\nno v\n\n4. Potentially P/vP: Further information needed if substance also potentially B**. Develop a testing strategy for simulation testing\n\n{ OECD TG 309 technically feasible?) no I yes v\n\n4.1 Is there compartment specific concern for soil or sediment? - water compartment is not at all relevant (based on fate and compartment of release), - persistence criteria are most likely to be exceeded in sediment or soil, - High hydrolysis rate etc...\n\nStart with OECD TG 309 (freshwater or marine water)\n\nL yes\n\n(OECD TGs 308, 307, or other) and test conditions - technical aspects - compartment of concern aspects 4.2 Specify/ justify the test compartment\n\n5. Further information needed for P- assessment for the chosen compartment (e.g. on specific degradation products)?\n\nyes\n\nCo no ves v\n\n7. Conclude P & vP assessment for the compartment\n\nv\n\n6. Can the result(s) be used, together with other relevant available information, to conclude persistence in the remaining compartment(s)?\n\nChoose the next compartment for testing\n\nyes\n\n7. Conclude P and/or vP assessment for all compartments\n\n*This conclusion (not P/vP) can not be drawn if the Weight-of-Evidence assessment indicates a P concern. ** In the context of the Biocidal Product Regulation (BPR), it is worth noting that the P-criteria has to be assessed also when the T-criterion is (potentially) fulfilled.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "0cebdf60-bb92-4c63-b521-d5b33af89869", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "information_requirements_r11_en.pdf"}}